const doors = [
 {id:"origins",name:"Growing Up",subtitle:"The origins of your sensibility",color:"#d9b47b",intro:"Before you knew what cinema was, it was already part of your world. What has stayed with you, and what has changed?",prompts:["What film from childhood has never quite left you?","What do you remember about the circumstances, the feeling, or the image?","Does that memory resemble something you value today, or is the connection less clear?"],challenge:"Could familiarity and cultural exposure explain this preference just as well as a formative experience?",science:"Autobiographical memory is reconstructed, not replayed. Childhood associations can influence later preferences, but a vivid memory does not establish causation.",networks:"Hippocampus · medial prefrontal cortex · autobiographical memory",source:"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.neuropsychologia.2007.09.023",reflection:"What have you carried from childhood without ever deciding to carry it?"},
 {id:"people",name:"People & Memories",subtitle:"The lives beside yours",color:"#d99ba9",intro:"Sometimes the film is almost incidental. A person, a place, a shared silence becomes part of the story.",prompts:["Which movie brings someone in your life to mind?","What belongs to the film, and what belongs to the person or the circumstances?","How has your relationship to that memory changed?"],challenge:"Is cinema a way of connecting with people, a substitute for connection, or neither in this instance?",science:"Remembering personal events and thinking about other people recruit overlapping distributed networks. A film can act as a cue for a relational memory.",networks:"Hippocampus · medial prefrontal cortex · posterior cingulate · temporoparietal junction",source:"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.neuropsychologia.2007.09.023",reflection:"When you miss a film, what exactly are you missing?"},
 {id:"canon",name:"My Masterpieces",subtitle:"The things you value",color:"#b8a0e6",intro:"A masterpiece need not be your favorite film. It might embody a standard of beauty, truth, craft or humanity that you hold dear.",prompts:["Name a film that represents what cinema can be for you.","What specifically makes it extraordinary to you?","Do those qualities matter outside cinema, or are they specific to art?"],challenge:"How much of your admiration is your experience of the film, and how much might be influenced by reputation, education or the identity of being a cinephile?",science:"Aesthetic appreciation involves perceptual, valuation and self-referential processes. Research links intense aesthetic experiences to activity in parts of the default mode network; it does not read an individual's values from a film preference.",networks:"Visual association cortex · valuation networks · default mode network",source:"https://doi.org/10.3389/fnhum.2012.00066",reflection:"Do you live by the qualities you admire on screen? Do you need to?"},
 {id:"needs",name:"When I Needed Cinema",subtitle:"What you were seeking",color:"#89b8df",intro:"The desire to go to a movie and the desire to watch one are not always the same. What did you need from the experience?",prompts:["Recall a time when you turned to cinema, whether happy, sad, restless or otherwise.","What did you expect to feel before you went? What actually happened?","What other activity might have met that need? What made cinema different?"],challenge:"Was cinema helping you engage with a feeling, avoid it, follow a habit, or something else? What evidence distinguishes those possibilities?",science:"Motivational wanting and experienced liking can diverge. Dopamine participates in reward learning and motivation, but is not a simple measure of pleasure. Your account cannot reveal your neurotransmitter levels.",networks:"Striatum · prefrontal networks · insula · amygdala",source:"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.neuron.2015.02.009",reflection:"Before your next movie, notice what you want from it. Afterwards, notice what you actually received."},
 {id:"discovery",name:"Discoveries",subtitle:"The ways you have changed",color:"#87c6b6",intro:"Some films introduce a new world. Others make the familiar world impossible to see in quite the same way.",prompts:["What film changed how you saw something, or made you reconsider a belief?","What did you believe before, and what changed afterward?","Did anything in your behavior change, or only your thinking? It is fine if nothing did."],challenge:"Could this have been a temporary feeling of insight rather than a lasting change?",science:"Curiosity can enhance learning and memory. Research implicates hippocampal and midbrain reward systems, but exposure to a new perspective does not by itself demonstrate changed beliefs or behavior.",networks:"Hippocampus · midbrain reward systems · frontoparietal networks",source:"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.neuron.2014.08.060",reflection:"What has cinema actually changed about the way you live?"},
 {id:"moments",name:"Unforgettable Moments",subtitle:"What remains",color:"#e1a17a",intro:"A glance, a sound, an image. You may have forgotten the plot, but something remains.",prompts:["Describe a cinematic moment you still carry with you.","What do you remember feeling then, and what do you feel now?","Is the significance in the scene, your life at the time, or something you cannot yet explain?"],challenge:"Does the memory need a deeper meaning? Could sensory novelty, repetition or coincidence be enough?",science:"Emotion and attention can influence memory encoding and retrieval. The amygdala interacts with memory systems, but vividness is not proof of accuracy or hidden significance.",networks:"Amygdala · hippocampus · sensory association cortex",source:"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.tics.2010.03.002",reflection:"What remains when the story itself has faded?"}
];
